<ProseBlock
  heading="Tools for merchants"
  layout="stacked"
  image={businessHome}
  imageAlt="The Business Home welcome screen, with shortcuts to create content, manage locations, and view analytics, beside “What’s next” cards to turn a single image into a photoshoot and to review brand details"
  imageCaption="Business Home, Google’s next-generation merchant experience."
>
  <p>
    In 2026, I took a contract with a Google team working on generative-AI image
    tools for Merchants. That summer, our team’s main project was to launch
    Business Home, Google’s next-generation merchant experience. Business Home
    aims to bring Google’s suite of tools for merchants and small businesses
    into a single platform, with AI content and photo tools, location
    management, growth guidance, and customer messaging. It launched as a <a
      href="https://support.google.com/businesshome/answer/17190673?hl=en"
      >closed beta</a
    > for select businesses in the US and UK.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock
  heading="Prototyping with AI"
  image={aiCodeEditor}
  imageAlt="The Antigravity IDE with the prototyping-kit repository open: its folders in the file explorer, and an agent panel asking “Welcome back, Morgan. Where should we pick up?” above a prompt box set to Gemini 3.8 Flash"
  imageCaption="Google’s AI-coding tool, Antigravity."
>
  <p>
    At the same time, Google was encouraging designers to adopt its AI code
    editor, known publicly as Antigravity. Designers were experimenting with
    Gemini and Antigravity to build working prototypes along side their regular
    design work. The promise was speed and higher fidelity flows for review and
    testing. In practice, it was still messy.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock heading="My role">
  <p>
    I was a UX Designer II, filling in for an L5 Sr designer on the Creative
    Content team, which worked on Google’s Merchant Center. When I first
    started, I owned the “Discovery & Engagement” pillar of Product Studio, a
    new image-generation flow to help users improve the quality and consistency
    of their product-image assets.
  </p>
  <p>
    For Business Home, we were translating Product Studio to an entirely new
    platform and I collaborated with two other designers to recreate the primary
    flows. Over the course of this ~8-week project, I designed and built an AI
    prototyping kit myself, alongside my design work on it. That’s how I like to
    work: constantly improving my process, picking up new tools and skills as I
    go, always pushing what I can produce.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock
  heading="The first sandbox"
  image={singleHtmlFile}
  imageAlt="A single HTML file with every style packed into its head, beside two supporting files, design context in Markdown and a validation script, and the words “Ballooning file size and token usage…”"
  imageCaption="One file holding everything meant every change touched everything."
>
  <p>
    Our Design Manager and another Lead UX Designer set out to build a shared
    code sandbox where designers could prototype with AI. When my teammates and
    I tried the first version, we ran into some persistent problems: UI did not
    follow established styles closely, features would randomly change or
    regress, and simple prompts could result in excessively long generation
    turns.
  </p>
  <p>
    When I dug into the kit, I realized the problem was likely a a lack of
    structure. The sandbox contained a single HTML file with every style, view,
    and function/script embedded. Each request required the agent to read the
    entire prototype. This likely bloated agent context, leading to long turns.
    The lack of compartmentalization also meant a small change or hallucination
    could ripple through the entire prototype and lead to unexpected changes.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock
  heading="A different path"
  image={kitFolders}
  imageAlt="The kit’s four top-level folders: Agents for a consistent workflow, with scripts to review code and utilities for managing the repo; Code for compartmentalized, disposable code that keeps the repo approachable and contains dev minutiae; Docs for UX context as a first-class citizen, with the design system, user context, and content guidelines; and Export for easy sharing of versioned prototypes through an internal hosting tool"
  imageCaption="Separating concerns gave the agent less to read and less to break."
>
  <p>
    Over the summer the sandbox team kept iterating, focused on centralizing the
    sandbox and syncing it with production code. Those were the right goals for
    them, but as a contractor I did not have access to the monorepo where
    production code lived. So I decided to build my own kit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Though I was building it mainly for myself, but I wanted to create something
    that other designers to pick up easily—in case I got the chance to
    contribute to the team’s overall process.
  </p>
  <p>
    Years of working with startups and frontend teams, and working on my own
    projects, had given me a working sense of how good codebases are organized.
    I wanted to bring that structure to my kit in order to help agents stay on
    track.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock heading="Starting with a conversation">
  <p>
    I started the way I’d start with an engineering partner: by talking it
    through. I laid out what prototyping needs, above all speed and flexibility.
    I listed the interactions that would need dynamic data or services, and
    asked Gemini to recommend frameworks and an architecture. It suggested Vite
    for its speed and flexibility.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock
  heading="One library, many teams"
  layout="stacked"
  image={guardrails}
  imageAlt="The four guardrails, agents.md, tokens.json, components, and linting, stacked above an abstract burst of horizontal lines, with lines on either side converging toward it"
  imageCaption="When the library changed in Figma, the kit could follow."
>
  <p>
    Many teams were working on different features and flows all at once. A team
    of visual designers was building our own fork of Material Design 3 as well.
    The prototypes coming in from other designers were biased toward vanilla M3
    components and styles. Because new components and styles were not in code
    format, and because the agent instructions cited M3 as a fallback, Gemini
    would often default to Material Design to fill in gaps. The prototypes were
    functional, and still good for user testing, but it was hard to reach high
    fidelity final outputs for engineering handoff. I realized that, while it is
    natural to want to give agents as much context and tools as possible,
    sometimes reducing context is helpful. After all, AI can create just about
    anything—the real challenge is creating something specific and on-brand.
  </p>
  <p>
    While building mock-ups with the team’s Figma library, I was impressed with
    how sophisticated the library was (and material design, from which it was
    forked). There was a complex system of contextual color variables that
    adapted to surface and dark/light modes, as well as extensive type and
    shape/spacing settings. It occurred to me that there was a a lot value not
    being utilized from our Figma library. Moreover, the project was highly
    design-led, meaning that the most up-to-date thinking was usually reflected
    in Figma.
  </p>
  <p>
    With this in mind, I created layers of integration to tie agent context to
    our Figma library and mock-ups. Figma’s design variables were exported as
    JSON tokens the kit could use directly. Agent skills, written from my own
    trial and error, told agents to carefully inspect a frame’s layer hierarchy
    and match its structure in code (to reduce rendering discrepancies) and
    forbid agent from using third-party libraries or non-token values. I also
    set up Figma Code Connect to map design components to their coded versions,
    as well as Storybook to spot-check component rendering and behavior.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock heading="Hiding the plumbing">
  <p>
    While I am personally comfortable with development tools (terminal, git,
    managing dependencies, running a dev server, etc) but not all designers
    are—and even for me those tools add overhead that can take away from my
    focus. There’s always a tension between a tool’s power and how much you are
    able to focus on the holistic picture. Coding by hand gives you total
    control, but you can easily spend hours on small details and lose a sense of
    how things hang together.
  </p>
  <p>
    I wanted the kit to handle the development busywork so that designers could
    spend more focused on the overall experience. So I wrote agent skills for
    common workflows: starting and serving the prototype, creating experimental
    branches and carrying updates between them, and exporting a build as a zip
    that could be easily uploaded to an internal sharing tool for testing and
    review.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

