<ProseBlock
  heading="Putting it to work"
  layout="stacked"
  image={mockData}
  imageAlt="Three kinds of mock data for the prototypes: Users, with persona info as mock user profiles that are switchable in-app; Businesses, by business category, such as retail versus a local restaurant; and Assets, context-specific complete sets of images per business, prompted with skill files"
  imageCaption="Mock data, dynamically served."
>
  <p>
    With all of this in place, I used the kit to prototype the flows for our
    upcoming Business Home launch. The difference showed in the details. My
    prototypes used only components and tokens from the new design library,
    creating near perfect alignment with mock ups.
  </p>
  <p>
    With the basics handled, I had room to push on interaction and other
    features. I explored ambient effects and special animations for a loading
    interstitial. I shared both with the team as inspiration—though we decided
    not to use it, it was a great example of pushing limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    I also added features to simulate context, including: mock user profiles,
    mock business profiles, custom images for each business, loading timers, and
    more. I even added an API key to feed real LLM responses into a simulated
    AI-assistant feature.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock
  heading="Rebuilding it on my own"
  image={kitReadme}
  imageAlt="The prototyping-kit README on GitHub: “A starting point for designers who prototype with AI agents,” with the kit’s idea, that Code is disposable, Agents evolves, and Docs is the source of truth, and the flow Figma to tokens to prototype and back to Figma"
  imageCaption="The independent kit, rebuilt from the ground up."
>
  <p>
    When my contract ended, the kit stayed with Google, but what I’d learned
    came with me. I rebuilt the kit from scratch, inspired by my work at Google,
    with no Google code or assets. In the new versioned I’ve explored
    compatibility with non-Google providers as well as generalizing features to
    work with other design systems and different workflows.
  </p>
  <p>
    The new kit is <a href="https://github.com/morgankeys/prototyping-kit"
      >public on GitHub</a
    >. I used it to <a href="https://github.com/morgankeys/morgankeysdotcomv3"
      >rebuild this portfolio site</a
    >, and I keep refining its skills and tools while I test new workflows and
    configurations. New challenges have arisen and it’s been exciting to explore
    possibilities.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock heading="Impact">
  <p>
    While I wasn’t in a position to steer the shared sandbox, the kit made an
    impression on the people I worked with most closely. Two designers directly
    tried it and came to me for tips and troubleshooting as they built their own
    prototypes. They told others about the kit.
  </p>
  <p>
    My Google point of contact asked me to walk a designer from another team
    through the kit, to help her think through her process. We also showed it to
    our engineers, who were struggling with fidelity and quick turnarounds.
  </p>
  <p>
    At the end of my contract, I handed the kit to my teammates. My
    point-of-contact even had me share with the broader Business Home
    team—hopefully influencing future sandbox improvements.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>
